  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying outcomes with probability > 1/n (n = number of outcomes) and then comparing the average number of such outcomes across lotteries. In this case, only the 0€ outcome in B (50%) qualifies as probable (since 50% > 1/3 ≈ 33.3%), while no outcome in A qualifies (25% and 75% are both ≤ 50%, and 75% is not > 50% in the context of the threshold). Thus, B has one probable outcome, A has zero. The individual, however, does not mention this threshold or the concept of probable outcomes in this way. Instead, the individual talks about the general probability of winning money (i.e., getting a positive amount), which is a different concept. The individual's justification is based on the chance of winning money (positive outcomes), not on the count of outcomes that exceed 1/n. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason as defined. The reasoning process described does not match the rule of the decision reason in terms of both the criteria (probability > 1/n) and the computation (average number of probable outcomes).",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
